==English==

===Etymology===
From Late {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|tīdung}}, from {{m|ang|tidan|tīdan|to befall, happen}}, probably with assimilation to {{term|-ing}}.<ref>T.F. Hoad, ''Concise Dictionary of English Etymology'', ISBN 978-0-19-283098-2; headword ''tidings''</ref>
Either from or influenced by {{etyl|non|en}} {{m|non|tíðindi}}<ref>{{R:Webster 1913|tidings}}</ref> ( > Danish/Norwegian {{m|da|tidende}}). Cognate with Dutch {{m|nl|tijding}}, German {{m|de|Zeitung}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{label|en|usually|in the plural}} [[news]]; new information
#* ''Glad '''tidings''' we bring To you and your kin.'' — A traditional Christmas carol.
#* '''1843''' [[:w:Thomas Carlyle|Thomas Carlyle]], ''[[:w:Past and Present (book)|Past and Present]]'', Book 2, Ch. 2, ''St. Edmundsbury''
#*: But yet it is pity we had lost '''tidings''' of our souls: actually we shall have to go in quest of them again, or worse in all ways will befall!

